        content: `[The Dark Projections: Categories and Layers of Hell] 
If we consult Sanskrit texts like Vasubandhu Bodhisattva's Abhidharmakośabhāṣyam, hell seemingly appears not as a concrete underground prison, but rather as an endless nightmare manifested by the extreme evil karma of sentient beings. Sutras and treatises often divide it into the "Eight Hot Hells" and "Eight Cold Hells."

Ranging from the initial "Sañjīva Hell" (where beings repeatedly die from boundless agony only to instantly revive) down to the deepest "Avīci Hell" (the hell of uninterrupted suffering). These extremes of heat and cold are perhaps the very projections of the violent hatred and extreme coldness within the minds of sentient beings. When these extreme emotions find no place to rest in the Dharma realm, they seemingly project naturally into the true illusions of mountains of blades, forests of swords, shelves of ice, and seas of fire.

[The Depth of Karma: The Three Grades of Ten Evil Deeds and the Five Uninterrupted Sins] 
Regarding why one falls into different evil realms, the treatises propose a measure called the "Ten Evil Karmas," dividing them into superior, medium, and inferior grades. This is perhaps not an external deity's judgment, but the concentration and attachment of the mind during the commission of the act.

Medium and Inferior Grades and the Realms of Animals and Hungry Ghosts: If evil is committed with a mixture of ignorance, craving, or slight unease and helplessness, this relatively weak and chaotic karmic force might draw the consciousness to wander into the eternally unfulfilled hunger of the "Hungry Ghost" or the obscure existence of the "Animal," which merely survives on instinct.
Superior Grade and Hell: If evil is committed without the slightest shame, harboring immense malice, or even taking pleasure in it, this pure and heavy darkness seemingly plunges directly into hell to endure endless torment.
The Five Uninterrupted Sins: As for the five gravest sins—killing one's father, killing one's mother, shedding the blood of a Buddha, or disrupting the harmonious Saṅgha—because they utterly sever the foundational grace of life and the vitality of awakening in the universe, the sheer magnitude of their reactionary force seemingly causes the consciousness to bypass all intermediate states and buffers, plummeting straight into the deepest "Avīci Hell," enduring suffering without a single gap in space or time.

[The Illusory Purity: The Layers of the Heavenly Realm and the Path of Sublimation] 
According to the framework of the Dīrgha Āgama and the Abhidharmakośa, the heavenly realm in the Dharma realm is seemingly not a single paradise, but is divided based on the purity of consciousness into the "Desire Realm," "Form Realm," and "Formless Realm," encompassing twenty-eight varying levels of heavenly states.

How to be Born in Heaven: If sentient beings in the world can uphold the "Ten Good Karmas" (no killing, no stealing, no sexual misconduct, no false speech, etc.), this pure goodness of refraining from evil can evoke the luminous and sorrow-free blessings of the "Desire Realm Heavens." If one can further cultivate "Dhyāna" (meditative concentration), letting go of gross attachments to the senses and matter, their consciousness can sublimate into the "Form Realm" or even the "Formless Realm," where not even a physical form exists.
The Limitations of Heaven: Yet, no matter how majestic and subtle the heavenly realms are, and no matter how vast their lifespans, as long as a trace of subtle attachment to "self" remains unbroken deep within the consciousness, one seemingly has not ultimately escaped this sea of stars known as saṃsāra. When blessings are exhausted, they will still fall like shooting stars.

[The Illusory Time: The Intertwining of Six-Realm Lifespans and the Sahā World] 
In the Buddhist cosmology, time is perhaps not an absolute length, but a subjective illusion experienced when the consciousness suffers or enjoys. The flow of time in each realm differs drastically from our human Sahā world.

Hungry Ghosts and Animals: One day in the hungry ghost realm is roughly equal to one month in our human world; the five hundred years they endure in endless hunger and thirst are terrifyingly long. The lifespans of animals are the most varied—the shortest are like microscopic insects born in the morning and dying at dusk, while the longest, like the great Dragon Kings, can live for an entire eon, all wasting away in the fog of ignorance.
The Length of Heaven: The flow of time in the heavenly realms is extremely slow. For instance, in the Heaven of the Four Great Kings, the lowest in the Desire Realm, one day equals fifty human years; for higher-level devas, their lifespans are easily measured in tens of thousands of eons, seemingly coeval with the universe.
The Endlessness of Hell: Most suffocating is the time in hell. Thousands of human years might merely be a brief instant for a hell-being enduring extreme punishment. In that abyss devoid of hope, every second of agony is infinitely stretched, and billions of years seemingly pass only in waiting for karma to burn away amidst repeated shattering and rebirth.

[Shadows of Karma: The Subtle Flow of Present, Fruitional, and Residual Retributions] 
When sentient beings commit the ten good or evil karmas in the world, the impact is seemingly not a single conclusion, but spreads layer upon layer across boundless time and space like ripples. Sutras and treatises often state that at the onset of committing evil, one might attract various misfortunes and disasters in their current life; this is the "Present Retribution" (Puṣpa/Flower), like a withered flower before the evil fruit forms. Once the physical body perishes, the consciousness, pulled by karma, falls directly into the three evil paths; this is the "Fruitional Retribution" (Phala). Even after the punishment in the evil paths is served and one regains a human form through transitions, they might still bear conditions of poverty, physical defects, or adversity; this is the "Residual Retribution" (Niṣyanda).

For us to be born as humans today, the manifestations of our birth are truly subtly intertwined. Obtaining this human form is fundamentally the "Fruitional Retribution" of past precepts and good deeds; however, the various sufferings we experience in this life are mostly the "Residual Retributions" left over from past evil karma.

[Good and Evil Do Not Offset: The Natural Trajectory of the Dharma Realm's Automatic Settlement] 
People of the world often hold the misconception that doing good can directly cancel out evil karma. Yet, in the underlying operations of the Dharma realm, good and evil seemingly follow their own respective tracks, possessing an exquisitely precise natural law of their own. Even if sincere repentance arises, it might only alter the speed or external conditions of the retribution, but that seed of karma, before encountering its activating conditions, seemingly will not vanish out of nowhere. Whether fruitional or residual retributions manifest first entirely depends on the flow of causes and conditions.

As revealed in the Lotus Sutra, depending on the depth of the ten evil karmas committed by sentient beings, they seemingly naturally manifest the fruitional retributions of the "three grades of evil paths"—hell, hungry ghosts, and animals. As mentioned in the Avataṃsaka Sutra, even if one regains a human form, if the residual habits of evil karma remain unexhausted, it will still evoke various residual retributions such as illness and a short life. The cosmic calculation's record of good and evil seemingly never misses a single detail.

[The Three Realms as Testing Grounds: Only by Arousing Bodhicitta Can One Escape Birth and Death] 
If one merely relies on cultivating the ten good deeds, even if they evoke the blessings of heaven, it is but exchanging one testing ground for another that is more enchanting and more prone to eroding the mind of the Way. As long as one remains trapped within the three realms, no matter which path they reside in, they are inevitably subjected to "Fractional Birth and Death" (Cyuti-upapatti)—the cyclic flow where the physical body repeatedly arises and perishes, and memories are washed away time and again.

To seek true escape, it seems the only way is to arouse the unsurpassed Bodhicitta, aligning the frequency of one's consciousness with Amitābha Buddha's Pure Land of Ultimate Bliss. Only by being received by the Buddha's vow power and born by transformation in that land can one truly and instantly sever this endless "Fractional Birth and Death."

【Note 1】: Definition of the Ten Good and Ten Evil Karmas
The ten evil karmas referred to in Buddhism encompass three dimensions: body, speech, and mind.
Three karmas of the body: Killing (harming life), Stealing (taking what is not given), Sexual Misconduct (lust that violates ethics).
Four karmas of speech: False Speech (deceitful words), Divisive Speech (sowing discord), Harsh Speech (hurting others with words), Idle Talk (frivolous and useless speech).
Three karmas of the mind: Greed (insatiable craving), Anger (resentment and malice), Ignorance (blindness to the law of cause and effect).
Distancing oneself from and eradicating these ten evil acts is termed the "Ten Good Karmas." This is the most foundational karmic education in the Dharma realm; understanding this principle allows one to know that every arising thought has an echo, thereby cultivating a mind of reverence.

【Note 2】: Regarding "Fractional Birth and Death" and "Transformational Birth and Death"
The principles of these two types of birth and death originate from the Mahayana text, the Śrīmālādevī Siṃhanāda Sūtra. The text categorizes the wandering of sentient beings into two types: "Fractional Death" (Cyuti) and "Inconceivable Transformational Death" (Acintyā pāriṇāmikī cyuti). "Fractional Death" is the birth-and-death saṃsāra of ordinary beings within the three realms, bound by karma, physical forms, and lifespan limits. "Inconceivable Transformational Death" is the metamorphic state of Bodhisattvas who, having transcended the three realms, eradicate ignorance and undergo subtle sublimations of consciousness on the path to Buddhahood.`
